
                    libyaz0 -- notes for contributors

Licensing of Contributions

    Submissions are accepted under the GNU Lesser General Public License
    version 3 or later. New files should include the standard header that is
    present in every file.

Testing libyaz0

    libyaz0 aims to be a fundamental library upon which larger libraries and
    applications are built. As such, it is very important that the library is
    thoroughly tested. Just testing against a suite of synthetic tests is
    insufficient, which is why it is recommended that you always test against
    a suite of real world data before making a pull request.

    To help contributors, there's a utility that helps you extract Yaz0 files
    at test/utils/extract_rom.py. You can invoke this utility against a
    Nintendo 64 Zelda ROM together with its decompressed version to extract
    Yaz0 files and their decompressed counterparts.

    Acquire a Nintendo 64 Zelda ROM, and a known good ROM decompressor (like
    Decompress that is shipped with the Ocarina of Time Randomizer), and then:

        known-good-decompressor path/to/rom.z64 path/to/rom-decompressed.z64
        python extract_rom.py path/to/rom.z64 \
            path/to/rom-decompressed.z64      \
            path/to/test/files

    Then, run CMake to generate a build file like this:

        cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -S . -B path/to/build-dir \
            -DYAZ0_TEST_DATA_DIR=path/to/test/files
        cd path/to/build-dir
        ctest --output-on-failure

    Be advised that the test suite may take a few minutes. For example, if the
    test suite runs against an extracted Ocarina of Time 1.0 (US) ROM it will
    run nearly 3000 combined compression and decompression tests.

    Note: YAZ0_TEST_DATA_DIR is scanned recursively. You can run tests against
    multiple ROMs by extracting each ROM to a subdirectory.

    Note: We cannot help you acquire a Nintendo 64 Zelda ROM, you will have to
    acquire one yourself from a cartridge.

Troubleshooting Failed Tests

    One useful quality of extract_rom.py is that the file names for a specific
    ROM will always be the same for everyone. If you're working on a feature
    that has tests failing on a specific file from a ROM, you can share the
    name of the test and its results in an issue on the issue tracker, for
    example:

        ROM:  Ocarina of Time 1.0 (US)
        Test: CompressFile.OOT_1_O_US.042D

        <Results of the Test>

    This will allow other developers to reproduce the results.
